    "part": "PART III — FUNDAMENTAL RIGHTS",
    "title": "Article 26: Freedom to Manage Religious Affairs",
    "definition": "Subject to public order, morality, and health, every religious denomination or any section thereof shall have the right — (a) to establish and maintain institutions for religious and charitable purposes; (b) to manage its own affairs in matters of religion; (c) to own and acquire movable and immovable property; and (d) to administer such property in accordance with law.",
    "explanation": "Article 26 complements Article 25 by providing collective religious freedom to religious denominations and their sections. While Article 25 protects individual religious liberty, Article 26 protects the institutional and organizational dimensions of religion. It recognizes that religions function not merely as individual beliefs but as organized communities with institutions, properties, and collective practices that require constitutional protection.\n\nThe threshold question under Article 26 is: what constitutes a \"religious denomination\"? The Supreme Court in Commissioner, Hindu Religious Endowments v. Sri Lakshmindra Thirtha Swamiar (1954) defined a religious denomination as a body of individuals who share (a) a common faith; (b) a common organization; and (c) a designation by a distinctive name. Applying this test, the Court has recognized various groups as religious denominations — the Swaminarayan sect, the Ramakrishna Mission (though controversially), Ananda Marga, the Dawoodi Bohra community, and others.\n\nArticle 26(a) guarantees the right to establish and maintain institutions for religious AND charitable purposes. This includes temples, mosques, churches, gurudwaras, monasteries, ashrams, charitable trusts, educational institutions, and hospitals run by religious organizations. The conjunction \"and\" has been interpreted as \"and/or\" — institutions for purely religious purposes or purely charitable purposes are both protected.\n\nArticle 26(b) is the core of collective religious autonomy — the right to manage one's \"own affairs in matters of religion.\" This includes determining doctrines, conducting rituals, appointing religious leaders, deciding membership criteria, and all other matters that are purely religious in nature. The State cannot interfere in these matters. However, the crucial distinction between \"religious\" and \"secular\" affairs of a denomination has been the source of extensive litigation.\n\nThe Supreme Court has held that while the State cannot interfere in religious affairs under Article 26(b), it CAN regulate the secular affairs of a religious denomination, including its property management, financial administration, and employment practices. In Shirur Mutt case (1954), the Court struck down provisions of the Madras Hindu Religious and Charitable Endowments Act that gave the government excessive control over the religious affairs of mutts.\n\nArticles 26(c) and (d) protect property rights of religious denominations — the right to own, acquire, and administer property. However, Article 26(d) adds the qualification \"in accordance with law,\" meaning the State can regulate property administration through legislation. This has been used to justify state control over temple finances, audit requirements for religious trusts, and land reform laws affecting religious endowments.\n\nThe tension between Articles 25/26 and State regulation of religious institutions — particularly Hindu temples — remains one of the most debated constitutional issues. Hindu temples are widely administered by State-appointed boards (HR&CE departments), while mosques, churches, and gurudwaras enjoy relatively greater autonomy. Critics argue this differential treatment violates Articles 14 and 26.",
